@@ -0,0 +1,243 @@
+# DSolution 1.83" 2-Mic
+
+Bruce port for the **DSolution 1.83 inch 2-Mic** board (ESP32-S3 + NV3023 panel +
+ES7210 mic ADC + ES8311 speaker DAC).
+
+The retail device this board ships in is the **OSTB-2ST-WIFI** cube, which the
+ESPHome/Chinese community calls **"Xingzhi cube 1.83 2mic"**. That name matters: it is
+what makes an independent, working reference for every pin and for the panel
+init/transform available (see *References* at the end).
+
+```
+pio run -e dsolution-183-2mic            # build
+pio run -e dsolution-183-2mic -t upload  # flash (USB-C, native USB-CDC)
+pio device monitor                       # 115200
+```
+
+## Hardware
+
+| Block | Part | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| MCU | ESP32-S3, 16 MB QSPI flash, 8 MB octal PSRAM @80 MHz | `qio_opi`, `custom_16Mb.csv` |
+| Display | 1.83" IPS, **NV3023** (NewVision) | 240x284 glass, 240x320 controller RAM, 4-wire SPI |
+| Mic ADC | ES7210 @ I2C **0x40** | 2 mics, MIC3 carries the ES8311 echo loopback (AEC reference) |
+| Speaker DAC | ES8311 @ I2C **0x18** | I2S slave, MCLK from the ESP32 |
+| Amplifier | NS4150 class-D | enabled by PA Enable, **GPIO4 = HIGH** |
+| LED | 1x WS2812 | GPIO48, GRB |
+| Buttons | Boot (0), Vol+ (39), Vol- (40) | all active LOW |
+| Power | charge detect 47, battery ADC **17** (1:2 divider, ADC2 ch6) | GPIO38 in the vendor sheet is wrong |
+
+### Pinout
+
+| Function | GPIO | | Function | GPIO |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| LCD CS | 14 | | I2C SDA (codecs) | 12 |
+| LCD DC | 8 | | I2C SCL (codecs) | 11 |
+| LCD RES | 18 | | I2S MCLK | 5 |
+| LCD SCL/CLK | 9 | | I2S BCLK | 15 |
+| LCD SDA/MOSI | 10 | | I2S WS/LRCK | 16 |
+| LCD BL (PWM) | 13 | | I2S DIN (ES7210 -> ESP32) | 7 |
+| LED WS2812 | 48 | | I2S DOUT (ESP32 -> ES8311) | 6 |
+| Button Boot/Sel | 0 | | PA Enable | 4 |
+| Button Vol+ | 39 | | Charge detect | 47 |
+| Button Vol- | 40 | | Battery ADC | 17 |
+| (sleep ctrl) | 21 | | | |
+
+## Display (NV3023)
+
+The NV3023 is **not** an ST7789. It only accepts register writes after the `0xFD`
+unlock pages are selected, and its power/gamma tables are its own; an ST7789 init
+sequence (LovyanGFX `Panel_ST7789`, TFT_eSPI `ST7789_DRIVER`, Arduino_GFX
+`Arduino_ST7789`) leaves the panel black. DisplayDrivers has no NV3023 profile, so
+this board ships its own panel class:
+
+* `NV3023_Panel.h` — `lgfx::Panel_NV3023 : public lgfx::Panel_LCD`, carrying the
+  vendor init table verbatim plus the MADCTL table (rotation -> MV/MX/MY, BGR bit).
+* `include/DisplayDrivers_User_Setup.h` includes it when `DSOLUTION_183_2MIC` is set.
+  That header is the documented DisplayDrivers extension point and the only
+  project-owned file read early enough for `LOVYAN_PANEL` to resolve.
+* The `.ini` selects the backend (`-DUSE_LOVYANGFX=1`, `-DLOVYAN_PANEL=Panel_NV3023`)
+  and describes the panel geometry (`TFT_WIDTH/HEIGHT/MEM_*`, `TFT_ROTATION`,
+  `TFT_INVERTION`, pins, 40 MHz SPI).
+
+Why LovyanGFX and not the Arduino_GFX default: Arduino_GFX builds its panels from a
+fixed factory table, so a board cannot inject a controller it does not know. LovyanGFX
+allows a panel class of its own without patching a library.
+
+Mapping of the vendor's panel table to this port:
+
+| Vendor | Here |
+| --- | --- |
+| Swap XY = true, Mirror X = false, Mirror Y = true | `getMadCtl()` index 3 = `MV|MY` (`0xA0`) |
+| Color order BGR | bit 3 (`0x08`) OR'd in `getMadCtl()` -- independent of `TFT_RGB_ORDER` |
+| Invert colors = true | `-DTFT_INVERTION=1` -> `Panel_LCD::setInvert()` sends `0x21` |
+| Offset X = 36 | derived by Panel_LCD (`memory_height - panel_height`); **not** set as `TFT_OFFSET_*` |
+| Frame 284x240, offset_width 36, offset_height 0 | same window as above: CASET 36..319, RASET 0..239 in landscape |
+| 240x280 panel, 284x240 effective | `TFT_WIDTH=240`, `TFT_HEIGHT=284`, `TFT_MEM_HEIGHT=320` |
+| SPI 40 MHz | `-DTFT_WRITE_FREQ=40000000` -- the rate the working ESPHome config uses; 80 MHz was tried first and is a suspect for the black screen |
+
+Orientation: `-DTFT_ROTATION=3` is the panel's *mounting* offset and `-DROTATION=0`
+the runtime rotation, so the board boots in the vendor landscape (284x240) and
+Config > Rotation cycles through the normal four orientations.
+
+The init table, the MADCTL value (`0xA8` = MV|MY|BGR) and the 36-pixel offset were all
+checked line by line against a working ESPHome config for this exact box
+(`mipi_spi`, `model: CUSTOM`, `data_rate: 40MHz`, `color_order: bgr`,
+`invert_colors: true`, `dimensions 284x240 offset_width 36`,
+`transform swap_xy + mirror_y`) — they match. So if the panel is still dark, the bus is
+the first thing to look at, not the register table.
+
+If the colours come out inverted or the wrong way round on a different production
+batch, the two knobs are `-DTFT_INVERTION` and the `| 0x08` in `getMadCtl()`.
+
+## Audio
+
+Init order follows the vendor's mandatory sequence — **I2C, I2S, ES8311, ES7210, PA
+Enable** — split across the two hooks Bruce calls:
+
+* `_setup_gpio()`: I2C on 12/11 (`sys_i2c`, 100 kHz), I2S pins
+  (`speaker_bus` = bclk 15 / ws 16 / dout 6 / mclk 5, `mic_bus` = bclk 15 / din 7 /
+  ws 16 / `MIC_TYPE_I2S_MSB`), and **PA Enable GPIO4 HIGH immediately**, so the amp is
+  never left floating.
+* `_setup_codec_speaker(true)` (called before playback): ES8311 bring-up at `0x18`
+  (reset, clocks, 16-bit I2S slave, DAC power, 0 dB digital volume), then PA Enable HIGH.
+* `_setup_codec_mic(true)` (called before capture): ES7210 bring-up at `0x40` — the
+  Espressif `esp_codec_dev` register sequence: reset, HPF, analog power, 16-bit I2S
+  slave, clock dividers for MCLK = 256 x fs, MIC1/MIC2 gain 24 dB, MIC3 (AEC
+  reference) 0 dB, then power-on.
+
+MCLK: the ESP32 is the I2S master, so MCLK follows the sample rate at a fixed 256x
+ratio — 6.144 MHz at 24 kHz (vendor table) and 12.288 MHz at 48 kHz, which is the rate
+Bruce's own audio paths run at (speaker playback follows the file, capture is fixed at
+48 kHz). The ES7210 dividers are therefore programmed for the 48 kHz row of the
+Espressif coefficient table; the 256x rule the vendor insists on is unchanged.
+
+The ES7210 needs an MCLK pin, and Bruce's microphone path never routed one. The small
+patch in `src/modules/others/mic.cpp` reuses `bruceConfigPins.speaker_bus.mclk` for the
+mic I2S channel when the board declared one (nothing else drives that pin while the mic
+runs). Without it the mic ADC stays silent.
+
+Volume: `bruceConfig.soundVolume` scales the samples in software; the ES8311 DAC
+volume is left at 0 dB (`0x32 = 0xBF`). The vendor's "42 dB" is the chip's *maximum*
+gain setting, not the default.
+
+ES7210 echo reference: MIC3 is the internal ES8311 -> ES7210 loopback, configured at
+unity gain. Nothing in Bruce consumes it yet (there is no AEC in the firmware), but the
+register setup keeps it clean if an AEC-enabled app is added.
+
+## Buttons
+
+Full-coverage 3-button multi-gesture driver (`-DHAS_6_BUTTONS=1` in `dsolution-183-2mic.ini`,
+custom debounced state machine in `boards/dsolution-183-2mic/interface.cpp`):
+
+* **Tap**: `Vol-` (GPIO40) = `Prev`, `Vol+` (GPIO39) = `Next`, `Boot` (GPIO0) = `Sel`
+* **Hold**: `Vol-` (360 ms+) = `Up` (repeats every 130 ms), `Vol+` (360 ms+) = `Down` (repeats every 130 ms), `Boot` (520 ms+) = `Esc` (one-shot)
+* **Combos**: `Boot+Vol-` = `PrevPage`, `Boot+Vol+` = `NextPage`, `Vol-+Vol+` = `Esc` (instant)
+
+When waking the screen from sleep/dim, the waking press is swallowed cleanly until released
+so it never triggers an unintended menu action.
+
+Note: `src/modules/others/mic.cpp` calls `gpio_reset_pin(GPIO_NUM_0)` when a capture
+session ends (a leftover from boards whose mic power rail sits on GPIO0). Here GPIO0 is
+the Sel button, so `InputHandler()` re-arms the pin (`pinMode(0, INPUT_PULLUP)`) once a
+second -- otherwise Sel would go quiet after the first recording.
+
+## Power / battery
+
+* **Charge detect (47)** — driven by the charger's open-drain status output; pulled up,
+  `isCharging()` returns true while it reads LOW. Flip the comparison in
+  `interface.cpp` if this board's charger is active HIGH.
+* **Battery ADC (17)** — `ANALOG_BAT_PIN=17` with `ANALOG_BAT_MULTIPLIER=2.0f`: a pin
+  dump taken from the stock firmware of this exact box puts the battery divider on
+  GPIO17 = ADC2 channel 6 through a 1:2 divider. The vendor sheet's "Battery Status:
+  GPIO38" cannot be right — the S3 has no ADC channel above GPIO20 — and an earlier
+  revision of this port used it, which is why the gauge read 0 %.
+* **Sleep control / power latch (`-DDSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN=21`)** — driven HIGH from the
+  very first instruction of `_setup_gpio()` and left HIGH during normal operation;
+  hardware-verified on the real device to keep USB/system power stable without dropouts.
+
+## Module trimming & 284x240 UI
+
+* **Native-only build (`-DDSOLUTION_NO_EXT_HW=1`)** — excludes external radio/sensor
+  modules that have no physical hardware on this board (`RF/CC1101`, `NRF24`, `LoRa`,
+  `FM`, `IR`, `RFID`, `GPS`, `WireTools`, `EthernetMenu`, `iButton`, plus unused
+  `lib_deps`), keeping all native ESP32-S3 subsystems (`WiFi`, `BLE`, `Audio/Mic`,
+  `BadUSB/HID`, `WebUI`, `Clock`, `Connect`, `Files`, and `BruceJS` with safe stubs).
+* **Default theme & Grid layout** — boots in Cyber Cyan (`#00F0FF`, `0x079F`) + Ice-Teal
+  (`0x5577`) on black (`-DDEFAULT_MAIN_MENU_STYLE=1`) with an 8-item symmetrical 4x2
+  bento grid (`WiFi`, `BLE`, `Files`, `JS`, `Clock`, `Connect`, `Others`, `Config`) and
+  a framed `284x240` submenu layout with `[idx/total]` header, pill highlight, and
+  button legend footer. (If a prior `/bruce.conf` exists in LittleFS, clear it via
+  `Config -> Factory Reset` or set `UI Color -> Default` + `Main Menu Style -> Grid`.)
+
+## Files touched outside this folder
+
+Two one-line-ish hooks, both documented in place:
+
+1. `include/DisplayDrivers_User_Setup.h` — includes `NV3023_Panel.h` for this board so
+   `-DLOVYAN_PANEL=Panel_NV3023` resolves.
+2. `src/modules/others/mic.cpp` — routes an MCLK to `MIC_TYPE_I2S_MSB` capture when the
+   board declares one on its speaker bus (needed by the ES7210).
+
+## Status / what is not verified on hardware
+
+Compile status: the environment builds cleanly on CI (`.github/workflows/build-dsolution-183-2mic.yml`
+in the parent workspace repo, which applies the port to upstream Bruce at the pinned commit):
+
+```
+Flash: [===       ]  27.4% (used 4 601 355 bytes from 16 777 216 bytes)
+RAM:   [====      ]  39.8% (used 130 260 bytes from 327 680 bytes)
+firmware.bin          4 601 904 bytes     0 warnings from the board's own files
+firmware-factory.bin  4 667 440 bytes     bootloader+partitions+boot_app0+app, flash at 0x0
+NV3023 init table, MADCTL table and the board's _setup_gpio()/codec hooks are in the image
+```
+
+(CI run 44722383672 on `abd84f4`.)
+
+### First flash on real hardware -- what it showed
+
+The first build was flashed on the real box and came up **black, with a USB connection
+that kept dropping** ("chập chờn"). Two things caused that and both are now fixed here:
+
+1. **Identity.** The panel is an NV3023, and the vendor sheet this port was written from
+   (NV3030B label, battery on GPIO38, sleep latch on GPIO21) does not describe this
+   revision. The init table survived the correction unchanged -- it matches the working
+   ESPHome config for this board -- but the name, the battery pin and the SPI clock did
+   not.
+2. **Flashing the app image at 0x0.** `firmware.bin` is linked for 0x10000; writing it at
+   offset 0 overwrites the bootloader and leaves the ROM loader resetting forever. That
+   looks exactly like "black screen + flaky USB". The build now also emits one merged
+   image, `firmware-factory.bin` (~4.7 MB, not padded), which is the only thing to flash,
+   always at **0x0** -- and it is small enough to send through a browser WebSerial
+   flasher in a couple of minutes:
+
+   ```
+   esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash 0x0 firmware-factory.bin
+   ```
+
+### If it is still dead
+
+Do these in order -- each one produces a different symptom, so they pinpoint the fault:
+
+1. `esptool.py --chip esp32s3 erase_flash`, then write `firmware-factory.bin` at 0x0.
+2. Open the serial monitor at 115200 and reset the board. You should see the ROM banner,
+   then `[board] DSolution 1.83 2-Mic: NV3023 + ES7210/ES8311`. No banner but a repeating
+   ROM banner = the app crashes before `_setup_gpio()` (then it is PSRAM/flash config,
+   not the panel). Banner but no picture = the display bus (try
+   `-DTFT_WRITE_FREQ=20000000`, then check `TFT_BL=13` with a multimeter).
+3. Backlight is GPIO13 and it is *active HIGH* through `hal_bright_attach()`; if the panel
+   is lit but grey/white, the init table ran and only MADCTL/COLMOD are left.
+4. Recovery: the community keeps a **full 16 MB dump of the stock Xiaozhi firmware** for
+   this exact box (`ostb-2st`). Restoring it puts the device back to factory state:
+   `esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash 0x0 xiaozhi_ostb-2st_full16MB.bin`.
+
+## References
+
+* `RealDeco/xiaozhi-esphome` -> `devices/Xingzhi/xingzhi-cube-1.83-2mic.yaml` -- working
+  ESPHome config for this exact board: pins, `psram: octal/80MHz`, `USB_SERIAL_JTAG`
+  logger, and the full `mipi_spi` display block (init sequence, transform, offset, BGR,
+  invert, 40 MHz).
+* `ProD-byte/Maerchenbox` -- pin table "read out of the Xiaozhi firmware" for
+  `ostb-2st-wifi-double-mic`: NV3023 284x240 x-offset 36, battery ADC GPIO17 (ADC2 ch6,
+  1:2), plus the 16 MB recovery image instructions.
+* `espressif/esp_lcd_nv3023` (components.espressif.com) -- Espressif's own NV3023 driver.
